1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,240
Do you use any navigation application to go through traffic jams?

2
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:14,060
What happens when you scroll down through your social media feed?

3
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:20,080
Do you follow any recommendation that any streaming service might give you?

4
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:25,700
So by the end of the day, we know that AI is there, but do we actually understand what

5
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:37,300
is going on, and can we trust the output of those applications?

6
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:43,300
To date, the approach that we've taken to these kinds of technologies is to say, well,

7
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:45,520
consumers can figure this out on their own.

8
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,300
They can read the terms and conditions on websites.

9
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:54,100
They can choose not to participate in certain digital environments, but increasingly, these

10
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:57,620
products and platforms are part of our lives.

11
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:00,380
They are part of the way that we provide education.

12
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:03,380
They are part of the way that we look for jobs.

13
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:11,340
And that power imbalance cannot be addressed by simply giving consumers more information

14
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:14,900
or giving them individual rights of complaint.

15
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:20,580
What we're ultimately going to have to do if we're going to change the kind of structural

16
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:27,780
ways in which these technologies are designed is to push the responsibilities back on our

17
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:35,980
designers, back on the organizations who are relying on technologies to change their practices.

18
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:40,900
Technologies like artificial intelligence have the potential to empower people and to

19
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:49,000
broaden their perspectives, or they can widen inequalities and really don't contribute to

20
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,160
address our societal challenges.

21
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:53,680
But we cannot blame the technologies.

22
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:55,720
It's not about the technologies.

23
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:01,140
It's about us developing the framework, shaping the rules that will allow these technologies

24
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:05,320
to deliver to what we want them to achieve.

25
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:11,880
And we need to make sure that we embed those very important ethical principles, the protection

26
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:21,880
and promotion of human rights and human dignity that will at the end determine the outcomes.

27
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:28,840
Responsible governance of AI technologies without the buy-in of Big Tech and other companies

28
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:30,720
is impossible.

29
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:39,600
It is necessary for us to find ways in which to explain to them that ethics is not about

30
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,320
abstract principles.

31
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:43,840
Ethics should be bottom up.

32
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:45,960
It is a dynamic system.

33
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:51,720
It should enable innovation and in the end lead to trust in products of these companies

34
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:58,560
which would lead to success in terms of whatever their business aims are.

35
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:05,320
We see how ethical debates end up playing an important role in shaping the conversation

36
00:03:05,320 --> 00:03:09,680
of AI regulation over the last five years.

37
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:22,040
We saw a plethora of charters, declarations of AI ethical principles.

38
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:30,720
We are seeing right now that those principles are being applied on a very practical basis.

39
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:39,520
A lot of countries in Latin America have already come up with their national strategies on artificial intelligence.

40
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:43,000
But some countries in the region are going a step further.

41
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,360
They are actually regulating through hard law some of the principles of artificial intelligence.

42
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:55,440
In the European Union, we're looking at draft AI acts.

43
00:03:55,440 --> 00:04:02,840
In the US, Congress is starting to talk in great detail about monopoly power of technology companies.

44
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:13,920
And you can see the wheels turning across the world as countries move from awareness-raising

45
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:18,640
to strategy, to attempts at implementation, attempts at regulation.

